// Plotting demonstration for the \use{plot} package.
//
// Render with:  texish scripts/plot.script -o plot -p letter
//
// The plot package is built entirely in the document language over the \picture
// vector-graphics layer: \plot sets up a data-to-device transform, draws the axes,
// tick marks, numeric labels, grid and title, then runs its body to draw the data
// series on top. Series come in four kinds — \lineplot, \scatter, \bars and \fnplot.

\use{plot}

\def headline {\italic{Plot demo} \hfil page \the\pageno}
\def footline {\hfil — \the\pageno — \hfil}

\font lmroman 12 regular

\bold{\TeXish{} plots — data graphics in the document}

A \italic{plot} is a \picture with a coordinate system: you give the data ranges
and a few series, and the package maps data values to the page, draws the axes and
labelled ticks, and strokes the series. Because it is all the document language,
the prose, the axes and the curve share one rendering pipeline.

\bold{A line series}

\xrange{0}{6}
\yrange{0}{130}
\xlabel{time (s)}
\ylabel{height (m)}
\plottitle{Projectile}
\plot{
  \lineplot{royalblue}{0 0  1 50  2 88  3 112  4 124  5 126  6 118}
  \scatter{crimson}{1 50  3 112  5 126}
}

A \italic{\\lineplot} strokes a polyline through its data points; a \italic{\\scatter}
drops a filled marker at each point. Both read the same flat \italic{x y x y …} list.

\bold{A bar chart}

A \italic{\\bars} series draws a vertical bar from the axis up to each value, centred
on its x position. Here the y range is set so the bars have headroom.

\xrange{0}{7}
\yrange{0}{160}
\xlabel{quarter}
\ylabel{revenue}
\plottitle{Quarterly revenue}
\set plotgrid {1}
\plot{
  \bars{teal}{1 95  2 110  3 80  4 140  5 120  6 150}
}

\eject

\bold{A function plot}

\italic{\\fnplot} samples a function of \italic{x} across the domain and strokes the
result. The expression is ordinary \italic{\\calc} arithmetic, written in terms of
\italic{x}, so any function the expression evaluator knows can be drawn.

\xrange{-3}{3}
\yrange{0}{9}
\xlabel{$x$}
\ylabel{$y$}
\plottitle{$y = x^2$}
\plot{
  \fnplot{seagreen}{x*x}
}

\bold{Two curves and a fine grid}

A plot body can hold several series, and the tick step can be forced with
\italic{\\xstep} / \italic{\\ystep} when the automatic choice is not what you want.

\xrange{0}{6.283}
\yrange{-1.2}{1.2}
\xstep{1}
\ystep{0.5}
\xlabel{angle (rad)}
\plottitle{Sine and cosine}
\plot{
  \fnplot{darkorange}{sin(x)}
  \fnplot{mediumvioletred}{cos(x)}
}

Each figure above is a single box that flows in the text like any other, so a plot
can sit inline with prose, in a figure float, or beside a table.
